  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure will slowly build towards the \nregion through Wednesday. \n\nHigh pressure is expected to slowly build towards the region.\nHowever, the main mid and upper level low which was responsible\nfor the unsettled weather over the past few days, will still be\ncentered over Ontario and Quebec through Tuesday. As a result, \nwe could continue to see some isolated showers and storms, \nparticularly in the afternoon hours as embedded short wave \ntroughs pivot through the region through Tuesday before the main\nlow finally starts to lift northeast away from the region. \n\nGiven the proximity to the main trough, the main area that could\npotentially see showers and storms is in NJ and SE PA. The\nactivity is expected to generally stay north of Delmarva. At \nthis point, main concerns with any storms are cloud-to-ground \nlightning and brief heavy downpours, but the risk for other \nhazards is quite low.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A warm front on Thursday, will be followed very\nclosely by a cold front Friday into Saturday, resulting in \nunsettled weather at the end of the week. \n\nBy Thursday, high pressure moves offshore with southerly return\nflow behind a warm front, helping bring dew points back into \nthe low-70s. There looks to be two chances for showers and\nstorms. The first is expected to be Thursday with and behind the\nwarm front. Another chance for storms is possible Friday into\nSaturday as a cold front approaches. The timing of this cold\nfront will be critical in the risk for thunderstorm hazards, so\nuncertain on the severe and flooding risk at this point, but\nwill continue to monitor as we go through the week.",
